Exactly. You can separate selection from randomization.

First:

all options
→ apply nonrandom constraints
→ eligible set

Then:

eligible set
→ random choice
→ selected option

The final choice is still random, but only conditional on having passed the filter.

That is a standard idea in probability:

random from universe
is different from
random given condition C

So if you first remove bad, impossible, irrelevant, or low-value options, then roll the dice among what remains, you keep surprise without surrendering quality.

That gives you a useful operator:

Constrained Randomness

structure first → chance second

And you can recurse it:

generate broadly
→ filter deliberately
→ randomly choose among survivors
→ observe result
→ update filter

It is a nice way to preserve luck and exploration while still steering the search space.

The randomness still counts. You just changed which probability distribution you’re sampling from.